  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Execution profiles integrated into an optimizing C compiler can guide classic optimizations toward the program regions that run most often.",
      "short_title": "Profiles guide classic optimizations",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "An execution profiler can be integrated into an optimizing compiler to provide the compiler with run-time information about user programs. For each code optimization, the profile information along with information from static loop analysis is used to reduce the execution time of the most frequently executed program regions."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Weighted flow graphs expose hot paths by attaching execution counts to basic blocks and taken counts to control-flow arcs.",
      "short_title": "Weighted graphs expose hot paths",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "We define a weighted flow graph as a quadruplet {V,E, count, arc.count], where each node in V is a basic block, each arc in E is a potential control flow path between two basic blocks, count{v) is a function that returns the execution count of a basic block u, and arc-count(e) is a function that returns the taken count of a control flow path e."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Super-blocks represent frequently executed paths as linear basic-block sequences that can only be entered at the first block but may exit from any block.",
      "short_title": "Super-blocks isolate hot paths",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "A super-block has the following features. (1) It is a linear sequence of basic blocks B(i),i = l..n, where n > 1. (2) It can be entered only from B( 1). (3) The program control may leave the super-block from any basic block."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Trace selection followed by tail duplication converts frequently executed traces into super-blocks by removing control-flow entries into the middle of a trace.",
      "short_title": "Tail duplication forms super-blocks",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The formation of super-blocks is a two step procedure: (1) trace selection and (2) tail duplication. Trace selection identifies basic blocks that tend to execute in a sequence and groups them into a trace.",
        "After trace selection, each trace is converted into a super-block by duplicating the tail part of the trace, in order to ensure that the program control can only enter at the top basic block."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Classic scalar optimizations can be reformulated to operate on super-blocks and super-block loops rather than only on traditional basic blocks or whole loops.",
      "short_title": "Classic optimizations fit super-blocks",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Table 2 shows a list of classic code optimizations that we have extended to use profile information.",
        "The non-loop based code optimizations work on a single super-block at a time. The loop based code optimizations work on a single super-block loop at a time."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Tail duplication creates additional optimization opportunities because it can make an earlier operation the sole producer of operands used later in a hot path.",
      "short_title": "Duplication exposes producer relationships",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Duplicating the tail part of a trace to form a super-block eliminates control paths that enter the trace in the middle. For many op(x) and op(y) instruction pairs, op(x) becomes the sole producer of one or more source operands of op(y). Therefore, profile-based operation combining can find more opportunities for optimization than traditional operation combining."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "On the benchmark suite, profile-based super-block optimizations averaged a 1.15 speedup over the prototype compiler's globally optimized code.",
      "short_title": "Profile optimization speeds benchmarks",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
